verdict=IN-SCOPE: the approved plan's section 2 names this step in detail — archive 030 with an Outcome, give 042 its Built already. Docs and NOTES only, no source. Self-judged.
# Self-judged, and said so rather than implied.
# 
# The approved plan's section 2 names this step in detail: "030's bullet moves to
# the shipped archive with an `## Outcome`: Option 1 delivered, all six stages
# aimable, OWED empty; Option 2's named snapshots were rejected as an alternative
# and remain unbuilt, so say that rather than letting the title's second half read
# as unfinished work" and "042 becomes rank 1 and gains its `## Built already`
# before the first edit — the gate requires it of the next-up record".
# 
# That is the whole diff. NOTES.md moves 030's bullet to the shipped archive and
# ticks it; 030 gains its Outcome; 042 gains Built already.
# 
# It rides this release rather than a later one because 030's Outcome says the
# work shipped in 2.60, and that sentence is only true once main carries it. The
# archive and the release land in the same promotion or the record claims
# something main does not yet have.
# 
# Two things went further than a minimal archive would, both named rather than
# absorbed:
# 
# - 030's Outcome states what is NOT built — Option 2's named snapshots — and why
#   that does not leave the record open. The record's own title carries a second
#   half that Option 2 covers and Option 1 does not, so an Outcome silent on it
#   would read as unfinished work in flight.
# - 042's Built already records that an aim is a WEIGHT and not a value
#   (`dz * aimWeight(...)`, where `dz` is the whole-frame slider), read off
#   src/pipeline.ts rather than assumed. That is the gap between what shipped and
#   what 042 asks for, and a record that did not say it is a record that invites
#   its own implementer to read six shipped toggles as the job already done.
# 
# No source file is touched.
